Because the SCFNN can predict the response to electrostatic fields, we can use a highly efficient method to estimate the dielectric constant \({ }^{56}\). To do so, we compute the \(r\)-dependent Kirkwood \(g\)-factor, \(G_{\mathrm{K}}(r)\), with \(\mathbf{E}=0\) and \(\mathbf{D}=0\), where
\[
G_{\mathrm{K}}(r)=\left\langle\boldsymbol{\mu}_{1} \cdot \mathbf{M}_{1}(r)\right\rangle / \mu^{2},
\]
\(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{1}\) is the dipole of a water molecule at the origin and \(\mathbf{M}_{1}(r)\) is the total dipole moment in a sphere of radius \(r\) including the molecule at the origin. The composite Kirkwood \(g\)-factor,
\[
G_{\mathrm{Kc}}(r)=\frac{1}{3}\left[2 G_{\mathrm{K}}(r)_{\mathrm{E}=0}+G_{\mathrm{K}}(r)_{\mathrm{D}=0}\right],
\]
converges rapidly with \(r\) to a constant \(g_{\mathrm{K}}\), which is related to the dielectric constant through Kirkwood's relation for polarizable molecules \({ }^{56}\)
\[
\frac{4 \pi \beta N \mu^{2} g_{\mathrm{K}}}{V}=\frac{(\varepsilon-1)(2 \varepsilon+1)}{\varepsilon}-\frac{\left(\varepsilon_{\infty}-1\right)\left(2 \varepsilon_{\infty}+1\right)}{\varepsilon_{\infty}},
\]
where \(N\) is the number of water molecules, \(V\) is the system volume, \(\beta=1 /\left(k_{\mathrm{B}} T\right)\), and \(\varepsilon_{\infty}\) is the high-frequency dielectric constant that arises from electronic polarization; \(\varepsilon_{\infty} \approx 1.65\) for the SCFNN model, as discussed below. As shown in Fig. 5a, the composite correlation function plateaus to a constant value near a distance of \(6 \AA\), as expected \({ }^{56}\). By replacing \(g_{\mathrm{K}}\) in Eq. (6) with \(G_{\mathrm{Kc}}(r)\) and inverting, we can compute the effective distance-

